# Implementation Plan

## Tasks (ordered for TDD — acceptance test first)

### 1. Write @wip acceptance test for new log format
- Update features/log.feature with a new @wip rule for the narrative format
- Scenario: add a yak, run yx log, assert new 3-line format:
  - Line 1: narrative with author and yak name ("added")
  - Line 2: relative or absolute timestamp
  - Line 3: "event:" and "sha:"
  - Entries separated by ──── rule
- Update compact.feature log assertions for summary line
- These fail immediately — that's the point

### 2. Add commit_sha to EventMetadata
- Add `commit_sha: Option<String>` to EventMetadata
- Populate in git.rs get_all_events() from commit.id()
- Unit test: metadata includes sha

### 3. Add format_narrative() to EventFormat trait
- Add `fn format_narrative(&self, author: &str) -> String` to EventFormat trait
- Compiler forces implementation on all event types:
  - Added: "author added name" / "author added name under parent"
  - Removed: "author removed name"
  - Moved: "author moved name under parent" / "author moved name to root"
  - FieldUpdated: smart per-field (started/finished/reset/updated context/tagged/renamed/updated field)
  - Compacted: "author compacted the event stream (N yaks)"
- Unit tests for each variant

### 4. Add relative timestamp formatting
- Add chrono-humanize (or similar) dependency
- Create helper: relative string from timestamp, lean on library defaults
- Unit test it exists and returns something sensible

### 5. Rewrite display and use case
- Update UserDisplay::log_entry to accept structured data
- Implement 3-line format:
  Line 1: narrative (yak names bold/coloured)
  Line 2: timestamp (dim)
  Line 3: event: id  sha: short_sha (dim)
  Followed by: ──── rule
- No-color: same lines without ANSI
- Update show_log.rs to wire narrative, timestamp, sha
- Compacted: single summary line

### 6. Make acceptance tests pass
- Remove @wip tag once green
- Verify all other tests still pass: dev check

